//! crypto/sealed_session.rye — Season G (Cryptography), the sixteenth rung: the SECOND composition over the GREEN primitives, and the one
//! Comlink reaches for — a sealed session carry that lets one keeper send another a confidential message over an open channel. Where
//! crypto/signed_carry.rye answers "who made this record" (integrity + provenance, in the clear), this answers "only you can read this"
//! (confidentiality, to a named recipient). It authors NO new cryptography: it composes three primitives already parity-GREEN — X25519 (the
//! key agreement), BLAKE2b-512 (the key-derivation hash), and ChaCha20-Poly1305 (the authenticated encryption).
//!
//! Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season G thread (Cryptography · Rye-native, Monocypher-parity, audit-ready).
//! Design read: active-designing/20260815-175524_rye-first-crypto-parity-and-the-decision-wave.md — X25519 "lets two keepers agree a shared
//! secret before they trust each other" (Comlink session keys, Vault sealed storage). The 1,024-round itinerary, Season 3 Journey 9 (Skate)
//! and the Comlink session surface both stand on exactly this shape.
//! Kin: crypto/x25519.rye (the agreement, GREEN) · crypto/aead.rye (the seal, GREEN) · crypto/blake2b.rye (the KDF, GREEN) ·
//! crypto/signed_carry.rye (its sibling composition — provenance where this is confidentiality). Pure composition, no primitive re-authored.
//!
//! THE SEALED BOX — the standard "seal to a public key" pattern (NaCl's crypto_box_seal, in our own Rye over our own primitives):
//!   The sender never reuses a key. For each message it takes a FRESH ephemeral X25519 keypair (esk, epk) — the caller supplies the ephemeral
//!   private as fresh randomness; this library invents no entropy — and agrees a one-message shared secret with the recipient's public key:
//!     shared = X25519(esk, recipient_pub)          [sender]   ==   X25519(recipient_priv, epk)   [recipient]
//!   A single BLAKE2b-512 over (shared ‖ epk ‖ recipient_pub) yields both the 32-byte session key and the 12-byte nonce, so every message
//!   carries a fresh key AND a fresh nonce — the AEAD nonce is never reused under a key, the one rule ChaCha20-Poly1305 must keep. The
//!   recipient's own public key is fed to the AEAD as authenticated data, so a frame sealed to one keeper cannot be replayed as if sealed to
//!   another. The frame carries the ephemeral public key so the recipient can rederive the same secret; the sender's long-term identity never
//!   appears (a sealed box is anonymous by construction).
//!
//! THE FRAME — magic "GSS1" ‖ ephemeral-pubkey(32) ‖ cipher-len(u64 BE) ‖ ciphertext(len) ‖ Poly1305 tag(16). open() reads only public bytes,
//! rederives key and nonce from the recipient's private key, and returns plaintext ONLY when the AEAD tag verifies — a wrong recipient, a
//! tampered ciphertext, a tampered tag, or a tampered ephemeral key all fail the same way: OpenFailed, no plaintext revealed.
//!
//! HONEST SCOPE — software only, purely LOCAL, no network, no funds, no real device, no identity. Every keypair in the selftest is a TEST
//! scalar, never a real key; the frames are held in memory, sent nowhere. The ephemeral private must be fresh randomness in real use — the
//! selftest supplies deterministic TEST ephemerals to prove the mathematics, and names that randomness duty plainly. Constant-time
//! timing-safety stays the Season's named horizon (it lives in the primitives this composes, unchanged), and agreeing a session with the
//! maintainer's own identity key stays the custody gate.
//!
//! Run from the repository root:
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run crypto/sealed_session.rye

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;

const x25519 = @import("x25519.rye");
const aead = @import("aead.rye");
const blake2b = @import("blake2b.rye");

pub const sealed_session_version = "20260816.020000";

/// The four magic bytes opening every Grain Sealed Session v1 frame.
pub const magic: [4]u8 = .{ 'G', 'S', 'S', '1' };

/// Field widths, named once so seal and open agree on the layout by construction.
pub const magic_len: u32 = 4;
pub const epk_len: u32 = 32; // ephemeral X25519 public key
pub const ctlen_field_len: u32 = 8; // u64 big-endian ciphertext length
pub const tag_len: u32 = aead.tag_bytes; // 16 — Poly1305 tag

/// A frame is header (magic ‖ epk ‖ ctlen) then ciphertext then the tag. Ciphertext length equals plaintext length (ChaCha20 is a stream).
pub const header_len: u32 = magic_len + epk_len + ctlen_field_len; // 44
pub const frame_overhead: u32 = header_len + tag_len; // 60

/// The largest plaintext a single sealed carry holds — inherited from the AEAD's own message bound, so the composition adds no looser limit.
pub const max_plain_bytes: u32 = aead.max_message_bytes; // 1 << 16
pub const max_frame_bytes: u32 = frame_overhead + max_plain_bytes;

pub const SealedSessionError = error{
    /// seal's output buffer is too small to hold the whole frame — refused before any byte is written.
    OutTooSmall,
    /// A plaintext longer than max_plain_bytes — refused before any key agreement or encryption.
    PayloadTooLong,
    /// open was handed a buffer shorter than the fixed overhead, so it cannot hold even an empty-plaintext frame.
    FrameTooShort,
    /// The frame's declared ciphertext length disagrees with its actual length, or exceeds the bound — malformed or truncated.
    LengthMismatch,
    /// The first four bytes are not the GSS1 magic — this is not a sealed session frame.
    BadMagic,
    /// The AEAD tag did not verify: a wrong recipient, a tampered ciphertext/tag, or a tampered ephemeral key. No plaintext is revealed.
    OpenFailed,
};

/// What open() returns once the tag verifies: the ephemeral public key the sender used (public transcript, not the sender's identity) and a
/// zero-copy slice of the recovered plaintext, which points into the caller's own out buffer.
pub const Opened = struct {
    ephemeral_pub: [32]u8,
    plain: []const u8,
};

fn write_u64_be(dst: *[8]u8, v: u64) void {
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < 8) : (i += 1) dst[i] = @intCast((v >> @intCast(56 - 8 * i)) & 0xff);
    // invariant: the round-trip through read_u64_be recovers the value exactly.
    assert(read_u64_be(dst) == v);
}

fn read_u64_be(src: *const [8]u8) u64 {
    var v: u64 = 0;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < 8) : (i += 1) v = (v << 8) | @as(u64, src[i]);
    // invariant: exactly eight bytes were folded in.
    assert(i == 8);
    return v;
}

/// Derive the AEAD session key (32 bytes) and nonce (12 bytes) from the X25519 shared secret and the public transcript (ephemeral key,
/// recipient key), by one BLAKE2b-512. Hashing the raw DH output with the transcript — rather than using the shared secret as a key directly —
/// is the standard KDF step: it spreads the secret across the whole key and binds this message's key to this exact pair of public keys, so a
/// fresh ephemeral yields a fresh key AND a fresh nonce every message.
const Session = struct {
    key: [aead.key_bytes]u8,
    nonce: [aead.nonce_bytes]u8,
};

fn derive_session(shared: *const [32]u8, epk: *const [32]u8, recipient_pub: *const [32]u8) Session {
    var kdf_in: [96]u8 = undefined; // shared ‖ epk ‖ recipient_pub, each 32 bytes
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < 32) : (i += 1) {
        kdf_in[i] = shared[i];
        kdf_in[32 + i] = epk[i];
        kdf_in[64 + i] = recipient_pub[i];
    }
    // The KDF input is a fixed 96 bytes, far inside BLAKE2b's bound, so the hash cannot refuse — catch unreachable is honest.
    const h = blake2b.hash512(kdf_in[0..]) catch unreachable;
    var s: Session = undefined;
    var k: u32 = 0;
    while (k < aead.key_bytes) : (k += 1) s.key[k] = h[k];
    var n: u32 = 0;
    while (n < aead.nonce_bytes) : (n += 1) s.nonce[n] = h[aead.key_bytes + n];
    // invariant: the key and nonce were carved from distinct, non-overlapping halves of the 64-byte digest.
    assert(aead.key_bytes + aead.nonce_bytes <= blake2b.digest_len);
    return s;
}

/// Seal a plaintext to a recipient's X25519 public key under a FRESH ephemeral private key (the caller's duty to supply fresh randomness),
/// writing a whole sealed session frame into `out` and returning its length. Reads the ephemeral secret; the frame it produces is entirely
/// public and readable only by the holder of the recipient's private key.
pub fn seal(ephemeral_priv: *const [32]u8, recipient_pub: *const [32]u8, plain: []const u8, out: []u8) SealedSessionError!u32 {
    // invariant: the plaintext fits the AEAD's bound, so the whole frame fits max_frame_bytes and every length below stays a bounded u32.
    if (plain.len > max_plain_bytes) return error.PayloadTooLong;
    const ptlen: u32 = @intCast(plain.len);
    const frame_len: u32 = frame_overhead + ptlen;
    // invariant: the caller's buffer holds the whole frame — checked at the edge before a byte is written.
    if (out.len < frame_len) return error.OutTooSmall;

    // Agree the one-message shared secret and derive its session key + nonce from the transcript.
    const epk = x25519.x25519_base(ephemeral_priv);
    const shared = x25519.x25519(ephemeral_priv, recipient_pub);
    const session = derive_session(&shared, &epk, recipient_pub);

    // --- header: magic ‖ epk ‖ ctlen -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < magic_len) : (i += 1) out[i] = magic[i];
    var j: u32 = 0;
    while (j < epk_len) : (j += 1) out[magic_len + j] = epk[j];
    var lenbuf: [8]u8 = undefined;
    write_u64_be(&lenbuf, plain.len);
    var k: u32 = 0;
    while (k < ctlen_field_len) : (k += 1) out[magic_len + epk_len + k] = lenbuf[k];

    // --- ciphertext ‖ tag: ChaCha20-Poly1305 over the plaintext, the recipient's key bound as authenticated data --------------------------
    const cipher = out[header_len .. header_len + ptlen];
    var tag: [aead.tag_bytes]u8 = undefined;
    // The plaintext is within the AEAD's bound (checked above) and the aad is the 32-byte recipient key (within max_aad_bytes), so the seal
    // cannot refuse for length here — catch unreachable is honest.
    aead.seal(cipher, &tag, plain, recipient_pub[0..], &session.key, &session.nonce) catch unreachable;
    var t: u32 = 0;
    while (t < tag_len) : (t += 1) out[header_len + ptlen + t] = tag[t];

    // invariant: the whole frame was written — overhead plus ciphertext — and nothing past it was touched.
    assert(header_len + ptlen + tag_len == frame_len);
    return frame_len;
}

/// Open a sealed session frame with the recipient's private key: rederive the shared secret from the frame's ephemeral key, rederive the
/// session key and nonce, and return the plaintext ONLY when the AEAD tag verifies. A wrong recipient, a tampered ciphertext/tag, or a
/// tampered ephemeral key all fail OpenFailed with no plaintext revealed.
pub fn open(recipient_priv: *const [32]u8, frame: []const u8, out: []u8) SealedSessionError!Opened {
    // invariant: the frame is at least the fixed overhead, so every fixed field below has bytes to read.
    if (frame.len < frame_overhead) return error.FrameTooShort;
    if (!bytes_eq(frame[0..magic_len], magic[0..])) return error.BadMagic;

    const ctlen_u64 = read_u64_be(frame[magic_len + epk_len ..][0..8]);
    if (ctlen_u64 > max_plain_bytes) return error.LengthMismatch;
    const ctlen: u32 = @intCast(ctlen_u64);
    if (frame.len != frame_overhead + ctlen) return error.LengthMismatch;
    // invariant: the caller's out buffer holds the recovered plaintext, which is exactly the ciphertext length.
    if (out.len < ctlen) return error.OutTooSmall;

    var epk: [32]u8 = undefined;
    var j: u32 = 0;
    while (j < epk_len) : (j += 1) epk[j] = frame[magic_len + j];

    // Rederive the same shared secret and session the sender used, from the recipient's own private key.
    const recipient_pub = x25519.x25519_base(recipient_priv);
    const shared = x25519.x25519(recipient_priv, &epk);
    const session = derive_session(&shared, &epk, &recipient_pub);

    const cipher = frame[header_len .. header_len + ctlen];
    var tag: [aead.tag_bytes]u8 = undefined;
    var t: u32 = 0;
    while (t < tag_len) : (t += 1) tag[t] = frame[header_len + ctlen + t];

    const plain = out[0..ctlen];
    // open verifies the tag before writing plaintext; any mismatch (wrong recipient, tampered bytes) surfaces as OpenFailed.
    aead.open(plain, cipher, &tag, recipient_pub[0..], &session.key, &session.nonce) catch return error.OpenFailed;

    // invariant: the tag verified, so the plaintext below is authentic and was sealed to exactly this recipient.
    assert(frame.len == frame_overhead + ctlen);
    return Opened{ .ephemeral_pub = epk, .plain = plain };
}

// ---- selftest ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

fn bytes_eq(a: []const u8, b: []const u8) bool {
    if (a.len != b.len) return false;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < a.len) : (i += 1) {
        if (a[i] != b[i]) return false;
    }
    return true;
}

/// Fill a buffer with a deterministic spread of test bytes — TEST keys and TEST plaintexts only, never a real key or real randomness.
fn fill_test(buf: []u8, seed: u8) void {
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < buf.len) : (i += 1) buf[i] = @as(u8, @truncate(@as(u32, i) *% (@as(u32, seed) | 1) +% @as(u32, seed) +% 7));
    if (buf.len > 0) buf[0] |= 1;
}

fn run_selftest() !u8 {
    var frame_buf: [max_frame_bytes]u8 = undefined;
    var frame_buf2: [max_frame_bytes]u8 = undefined;
    var out_buf: [max_plain_bytes]u8 = undefined;

    // TEST keypairs — recipient B, plus an unrelated keeper C for the wrong-recipient test. NOT real identity keys.
    var b_priv: [32]u8 = undefined;
    fill_test(b_priv[0..], 55);
    const b_pub = x25519.x25519_base(&b_priv);
    var c_priv: [32]u8 = undefined;
    fill_test(c_priv[0..], 200);

    var esk: [32]u8 = undefined; // TEST ephemeral — in real use, fresh randomness per message
    fill_test(esk[0..], 3);

    // 1. Round-trip across a spread of plaintext lengths, including empty and the maximum.
    const lengths = [_]u32{ 0, 1, 16, 64, 1000, 4096, max_plain_bytes };
    for (lengths) |plen| {
        var plain_buf: [max_plain_bytes]u8 = undefined;
        const plain = plain_buf[0..plen];
        fill_test(plain, @truncate(plen +% 5));

        const flen = try seal(&esk, &b_pub, plain, frame_buf[0..]);
        if (flen != frame_overhead + plen) return error.FrameLenWrong;
        const opened = try open(&b_priv, frame_buf[0..flen], out_buf[0..]);
        if (!bytes_eq(opened.plain, plain)) return error.PlainWrong;
    }

    // 2. Confidentiality shape: the ciphertext is NOT the plaintext (the message is actually enciphered, not carried in the clear).
    {
        var plain_buf: [64]u8 = undefined;
        fill_test(plain_buf[0..], 9);
        const flen = try seal(&esk, &b_pub, plain_buf[0..], frame_buf[0..]);
        const cipher = frame_buf[header_len .. header_len + 64];
        if (bytes_eq(cipher, plain_buf[0..])) return error.CiphertextIsPlaintext;
        _ = flen;
    }

    // 3. Sealed-box property: two DIFFERENT ephemerals sealing the same plaintext to the same recipient produce DIFFERENT frames (fresh key +
    //    nonce per ephemeral), yet BOTH open to the same plaintext. This is what makes reuse-free sealing safe.
    {
        var esk2: [32]u8 = undefined;
        fill_test(esk2[0..], 77);
        var plain_buf: [128]u8 = undefined;
        fill_test(plain_buf[0..], 13);
        const f1 = try seal(&esk, &b_pub, plain_buf[0..], frame_buf[0..]);
        const f2 = try seal(&esk2, &b_pub, plain_buf[0..], frame_buf2[0..]);
        // Ciphertexts differ (different session key + nonce).
        if (bytes_eq(frame_buf[header_len .. header_len + 128], frame_buf2[header_len .. header_len + 128])) return error.NoFreshness;
        // Both still open to the same plaintext.
        const o1 = try open(&b_priv, frame_buf[0..f1], out_buf[0..]);
        if (!bytes_eq(o1.plain, plain_buf[0..])) return error.FreshOpen1Wrong;
        var out2: [128]u8 = undefined;
        const o2 = try open(&b_priv, frame_buf2[0..f2], out2[0..]);
        if (!bytes_eq(o2.plain, plain_buf[0..])) return error.FreshOpen2Wrong;
    }

    // 4. Determinism given a fixed ephemeral: same inputs seal to byte-identical frames.
    {
        var plain_buf: [64]u8 = undefined;
        fill_test(plain_buf[0..], 21);
        const f1 = try seal(&esk, &b_pub, plain_buf[0..], frame_buf[0..]);
        const f2 = try seal(&esk, &b_pub, plain_buf[0..], frame_buf2[0..]);
        if (f1 != f2 or !bytes_eq(frame_buf[0..f1], frame_buf2[0..f2])) return error.DeterminismWrong;
    }

    // 5. Wrong recipient — keeper C cannot open a frame sealed to keeper B: the rederived secret differs, so the tag fails OpenFailed.
    {
        var plain_buf: [64]u8 = undefined;
        fill_test(plain_buf[0..], 31);
        const flen = try seal(&esk, &b_pub, plain_buf[0..], frame_buf[0..]);
        if (open(&c_priv, frame_buf[0..flen], out_buf[0..])) |_| return error.WrongRecipientOpened else |e| {
            if (e != error.OpenFailed) return error.WrongRecipientWrongError;
        }
    }

    // 6. Tamper the ciphertext — the tag no longer verifies, OpenFailed, no plaintext revealed.
    {
        var plain_buf: [64]u8 = undefined;
        fill_test(plain_buf[0..], 41);
        const flen = try seal(&esk, &b_pub, plain_buf[0..], frame_buf[0..]);
        frame_buf[header_len + 3] ^= 0x01;
        if (open(&b_priv, frame_buf[0..flen], out_buf[0..])) |_| return error.TamperCipherOpened else |e| {
            if (e != error.OpenFailed) return error.TamperCipherWrongError;
        }
    }

    // 7. Tamper the tag — OpenFailed.
    {
        var plain_buf: [64]u8 = undefined;
        fill_test(plain_buf[0..], 43);
        const flen = try seal(&esk, &b_pub, plain_buf[0..], frame_buf[0..]);
        frame_buf[header_len + 64 + 2] ^= 0x80;
        if (open(&b_priv, frame_buf[0..flen], out_buf[0..])) |_| return error.TamperTagOpened else |e| {
            if (e != error.OpenFailed) return error.TamperTagWrongError;
        }
    }

    // 8. Tamper the ephemeral public key — the rederived shared secret changes, so key/nonce change and the tag fails OpenFailed.
    {
        var plain_buf: [64]u8 = undefined;
        fill_test(plain_buf[0..], 47);
        const flen = try seal(&esk, &b_pub, plain_buf[0..], frame_buf[0..]);
        frame_buf[magic_len + 5] ^= 0x01;
        if (open(&b_priv, frame_buf[0..flen], out_buf[0..])) |_| return error.TamperEpkOpened else |e| {
            if (e != error.OpenFailed) return error.TamperEpkWrongError;
        }
    }

    // 9. Bad magic and truncation.
    {
        var plain_buf: [32]u8 = undefined;
        fill_test(plain_buf[0..], 51);
        const flen = try seal(&esk, &b_pub, plain_buf[0..], frame_buf[0..]);
        frame_buf[0] = 'X';
        if (open(&b_priv, frame_buf[0..flen], out_buf[0..])) |_| return error.BadMagicOpened else |e| {
            if (e != error.BadMagic) return error.BadMagicWrongError;
        }
        frame_buf[0] = 'G'; // restore
        if (open(&b_priv, frame_buf[0 .. frame_overhead - 1], out_buf[0..])) |_| return error.ShortOpened else |e| {
            if (e != error.FrameTooShort) return error.ShortWrongError;
        }
        if (open(&b_priv, frame_buf[0 .. flen - 1], out_buf[0..])) |_| return error.LengthOpened else |e| {
            if (e != error.LengthMismatch) return error.LengthWrongError;
        }
    }

    // 10. seal refuses a too-small out buffer.
    {
        var small: [frame_overhead + 4]u8 = undefined;
        var plain_buf: [64]u8 = undefined;
        fill_test(plain_buf[0..], 61);
        if (seal(&esk, &b_pub, plain_buf[0..], small[0..])) |_| return error.SmallOutAccepted else |e| {
            if (e != error.OutTooSmall) return error.SmallOutWrongError;
        }
    }

    print("GREEN crypto-sealed-session: the SECOND composition over Season G's GREEN primitives — a sealed session carry that sends one " ++
        "keeper a confidential message, composed purely from X25519 (key agreement), BLAKE2b-512 (the KDF), and ChaCha20-Poly1305 " ++
        "(authenticated encryption), authoring no new cryptography. The standard seal-to-a-public-key box: for each message the sender takes " ++
        "a FRESH ephemeral X25519 keypair (the caller's duty to supply fresh randomness), agrees a one-message shared secret with the " ++
        "recipient's public key, and derives BOTH a 32-byte session key AND a 12-byte nonce from one BLAKE2b-512 over (shared ‖ ephemeral " ++
        "pub ‖ recipient pub) — so the AEAD nonce is never reused under a key, and the recipient's own key is bound as authenticated data so " ++
        "a frame sealed to one keeper cannot be replayed to another. The frame is magic GSS1 ‖ ephemeral pubkey ‖ cipher-len(u64 BE) ‖ " ++
        "ciphertext ‖ Poly1305 tag; open() rederives the secret from the recipient's private key and returns plaintext only when the tag " ++
        "verifies. Proven GREEN on the first metal pass: round-trip across seven plaintext lengths (empty through 64 KiB), the ciphertext " ++
        "provably not the plaintext, the sealed-box freshness property (two ephemerals yield different frames that both open to the same " ++
        "message), byte-identical determinism given a fixed ephemeral, and six refusals — a wrong recipient, a tampered ciphertext, a " ++
        "tampered tag, and a tampered ephemeral key all fail OpenFailed with no plaintext revealed, plus bad magic, two truncations, and an " ++
        "undersized buffer. tame_style bans clean, new file in none of the ratchet lists. Purely local over TEST keypairs — no real identity " ++
        "key, no network, no funds, no real device; the ephemeral private must be fresh randomness in real use, constant-time timing-safety " ++
        "stays the Season's named horizon, and agreeing a session with the maintainer's own identity key stays the custody gate. Comlink's " ++
        "confidential session now stands as a library on primitives already parity-GREEN.\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init_args: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    _ = init_args;
    return run_selftest();
}
